## What we measure

For every output:

**Content preservation**

* WER
* CER
* substitutions/deletions/insertions

**Speaker identity representation**

* CAMPPlus similarity to the existing common target-speaker centroid

**Processing**

* processing/generation time
* output duration
* RTF

**Failure**

* looping;
* truncation;
* missing/repeated content;
* generation/conversion failure.

For STS we also calculate WER/CER on your **original six human recordings**, because that gives the ASR baseline. We then know whether a higher WER after conversion actually represents degradation rather than Whisper misunderstanding your original speech.

For speaker similarity we also retain the original-source → target score, so we can calculate:

```text
delta target similarity =
converted-output similarity
-
original-source similarity
```

That was one of the most useful findings from our previous STS work.

### What we will deliberately NOT measure

No:

* deception success;
* “would an employee believe this?” score;
* fake/real classification;
* statistical significance;
* general XTTS-vs-RVC-vs-Seed-VC ranking;
* arbitrary combined “quality score.”

And CAMPPlus will remain **speaker-embedding evidence, not proof of human speaker recognition**.

## Why this is much stronger

After this, Chapter 7 can make a very specific statement:

> Under fixed scenario-relevant German utterances, the three implemented approaches were evaluated for content preservation, target-speaker embedding similarity, processing time, and technical failure behavior. The evaluation does not estimate deception success; it tests whether the technical identity-presentation conditions assumed by the threat model can be instantiated by the tested implementations.

That is a **strong scientific demonstration** rather than a weak experiment.

The two sentences are also intentionally matched at **22 words each**, which removes one obvious confound when comparing their processing behavior.

